×1.3. That means about 100–200+ units left over, possible loss of deliveries when the 264-unit limit is hit, and higher storage costs. They lose thousands. Candidate 0's smaller final orders take only about 10 units off the leftover.\n4. **×1.1 vs ×1.0 (Candidate 6 vs 2).** I treated stock as a random walk over about 27 days with daily swings of about 8 units (item 0) and 14 units (item 1).\n   - Ordering ×1.1 saves about 28 units of item 0 and 42 of item 1 from going short. That is worth about 730 + 880 in sales and avoided penalties.\n   - But the extra purchases cost about 876 + 1260.\n   - Higher average stock adds about 600 in storage.\n   - Net: Candidate 2 comes out ahead by roughly 1,000.\n5. **Batch ordering.** Candidate 5 saves about 420 in daily order fees (30 per ordering day), but its bigger deliveries add about 390 in storage. On top of that it carries the same ×1.1 overstock, so it still trails Candidate 2. Candidate 4 (three-day batches) adds even more storage, and when large item-1 and item-0 deliveries land on consecutive days, the 264-unit limit could cut a delivery.\n\n**Robustness.** Candidate 2 holds the least stock, so nothing expires and the space limit is never at risk. Shortages from random demand swings are partly cushioned by the stock that builds up on its own. The cost of overstocking falls hardest on the larger plans."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4938, "cache_read_input_tokens": 844, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4938}, "output_tokens": 6, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-11-09T07:33:30.083Z", "request_id": "req_011CgrxuDGKgnQCoMRMyHen1"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop"